</Folder>
<Folder><name>Tour stops</name>
<Placemark><name>The D-Day Story, Portsmouth</name><description>Tour day 1

Portsmouth and the Solent were the heart of the D-Day build-up: troops, ships and landing craft packed the south coast in spring 1944, and Eisenhower's forward headquarters was at Southwick House just north of the city. This museum on Southsea seafront opened in 1984 as the D-Day Museum and reopened in 2018 as The D-Day Story. Its centrepiece is the Overlord Embroidery, commissioned by Lord Dulverton: 34 panels, 83 metres long, made by twenty members of the Royal School of Needlework over seven years. Outside is LCT 7074, the last surviving landing craft tank of its kind from D-Day.

Galleries follow D-Day from planning to the Normandy battle through personal stories, with a reconstructed Southwick House operations room. The Overlord Embroidery runs round its own gallery. Board LCT 7074 to see the open tank deck.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.089333,50.779800,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Churchill War Rooms, London</name><description>Tour day 2

The Cabinet War Rooms, in a basement beneath government offices off Whitehall, became operational on 27 August 1939, days before Germany invaded Poland. The War Cabinet met here 115 times, the last time on 28 March 1945, and Churchill made four wartime broadcasts from the site, the first on 11 September 1940. The Map Room, staffed day and night, tracked the war on every front. From 1943 a secure SIGSALY telephone link let Churchill speak to President Roosevelt from a room disguised as a private lavatory. The lights were switched off the day after Japan surrendered, and the rooms were preserved. They opened to the public in 1984.

Low corridors lead past the Cabinet Room, the Map Room with its original maps, coloured pins and telephones, the Transatlantic Telephone Room and Churchill's bedroom. The Churchill Museum, opened in 2005, covers his whole life.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.129028,51.502083,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Imperial War Museum London</name><description>Tour day 2

Britain's national museum of modern war has occupied the former Bethlem Royal Hospital on Lambeth Road since 1936. It is the best single place in Britain to see the Second World War as a whole: the Blitz, the war at sea and in the air, the home front and the campaigns from 1939 to 1945. In October 2021 it opened new Second World War and Holocaust galleries, which follow the war in Europe and the persecution and murder of Europe's Jews side by side. Outside stand two 15-inch naval guns, one from HMS Ramillies and the other mounted on HMS Resolution and HMS Roberts, which fired in support of D-Day.

Head for the Second World War Galleries and the Holocaust Galleries on the upper floors; the central atrium holds aircraft, rockets and vehicles, including a V-2 and a Spitfire. The two huge 15-inch guns frame the entrance.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.108662,51.495831,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bentley Priory Museum, Stanmore</name><description>Tour day 3

This country house was the headquarters of RAF Fighter Command throughout the Second World War. From here Air Chief Marshal Sir Hugh Dowding commanded the fighter force in the Battle of Britain and ran the defence system he had built: radar stations and Observer Corps reports fed into a filter room here, then out to group and sector operations rooms, which sent fighters to meet the raids. Dowding was removed from command in November 1940 but was made Baron Dowding of Bentley Priory in 1943. The RAF stayed until 2008. The museum opened in 2013 and tells the story of 'The One' (Dowding), 'The Few' (the aircrew) and 'The Many' who supported them.

The Italianate mansion's ground-floor rooms hold the museum, including Dowding's restored office looking over the gardens. Galleries on the aircrew and ground staff, a Battle of Britain timeline and a heritage trail in the grounds complete the visit.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.334175,51.626690,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>IWM Duxford</name><description>Tour day 5

Duxford became a fighter station in 1925, and in August 1938 its No. 19 Squadron was the first RAF unit to fly the Spitfire. In the Battle of Britain it was a sector station of No. 12 Group, guarding East Anglia and the Midlands and sending squadrons south to help 11 Group over London. From March 1943 the US Eighth Air Force's 78th Fighter Group flew from here, first with P-47 Thunderbolts and from December 1944 with P-51 Mustangs, escorting bombers and attacking ground targets until the war ended. The last RAF flight left in 1961, and the Imperial War Museum took over the site, which keeps many original wartime buildings.

Original hangars, the control tower and the restored Operations Block, pilots' briefing room and crew rooms show a working 1940 fighter station. Spitfire N3200 is on display, and Norman Foster's American Air Museum holds a B-17 and other US aircraft.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>0.129440,52.093060,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Biggin Hill Memorial Museum</name><description>Tour day 6

Biggin Hill was the best known of the 11 Group sector stations guarding the southern approaches to London, and in 1940 the Luftwaffe tried hard to knock it out. Between August 1940 and January 1941 it was attacked twelve times; the worst raid, at the end of August, hit the WAAF quarters and killed 39 people on the ground. After the war a public appeal backed by Churchill paid for St George's RAF Chapel of Remembrance, built on the site of a hangar destroyed in 1940; Lord Dowding laid the foundation stone in July 1951. The museum beside the chapel opened in 2019 and tells the station's story through the people who served there.

The brick chapel's twelve stained-glass windows by Hugh Easton each show a young airman with a squadron badge, and a tablet records airmen lost flying from Biggin Hill. Gate-guardian Spitfire and Hurricane replicas stand outside; the museum is next door.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>0.023428,51.327036,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>RAF Manston Spitfire &amp; Hurricane Memorial Museum</name><description>Tour day 6

Manston, on the tip of Kent, was the RAF fighter airfield closest to France and so one of the first targets in the Luftwaffe's August 1940 offensive against Fighter Command. At the height of the battle it was bombed almost daily and left littered with unexploded bombs, and many staff moved out to the nearby woods. Later it became a refuge for damaged bombers, fitted with the FIDO fog-clearance system, and in May 1943 No. 617 Squadron's Lancasters flew from here to drop inert test versions of Barnes Wallis's bouncing bomb off Reculver. The small free museum on the old airfield houses Spitfire XVI TB752 and Hurricane IIc LF751.

Two aircraft fill the purpose-built hall: Spitfire TB752, which flew in combat in 1945, and Hurricane LF751. Around them are displays on Manston's war and wartime Ramsgate. The café overlooks the former airfield. The separate RAF Manston History Museum is close by.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>1.349824,51.350246,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dover Castle Secret Wartime Tunnels</name><description>Tour day 7

Tunnels cut into the chalk below Dover Castle during the Napoleonic Wars were turned in 1939 into an air-raid shelter, then a naval command centre and an underground hospital. From here Vice-Admiral Bertram Ramsay planned and directed Operation Dynamo, the evacuation of the British Expeditionary Force and French troops from Dunkirk between 26 May and 4 June 1940. Working with a scratch fleet of warships, ferries and small civilian boats, his staff brought about 338,000 men back to England, far more than the 45,000 first hoped for. The operation is said to take its name from the room that had housed an electrical generator. The tunnels later served through the war as a headquarters guarding the Channel.

Guided routes take you through the tunnel complex: the Operation Dynamo galleries with projections telling the evacuation story, the operations rooms, and the reconstructed underground hospital. The medieval castle and Great Tower stand above, with views across the Channel to France on clear days.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>1.321400,51.129700,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Battle of Britain Memorial, Capel-le-Ferne</name><description>Tour day 7

On the cliffs between Folkestone and Dover, under the air battles of 1940, stands the national memorial to the aircrew of the Battle of Britain. It was unveiled by Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother on 9 July 1993. Its centre is Harry Gray's carved figure of a seated pilot looking out to sea; his flying jacket hides rank and nationality, so he stands for every Allied airman of 'The Few'. The Christopher Foxley-Norris Memorial Wall lists almost 3,000 aircrew who flew in the battle. The visitor centre, The Wing, shaped like a Spitfire's wing, was opened by Queen Elizabeth II on 26 March 2015.

From the air the memorial is a three-bladed propeller cut into the clifftop. Look for the seated pilot, the long wall of names, Churchill's words on the Beaverbrook Wall, and replica Hurricane and Spitfire. The Wing has the 'Scramble Experience' and a clifftop café.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>1.205181,51.098880,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dunkirk 1940 Museum (Bastion 32)</name><description>Tour day 8

Bastion 32 is a casemate in the old fortifications by the harbour, a short walk from the East Mole and the evacuation beaches. During the Battle of Dunkirk it served as the headquarters of the French and Allied forces defending the town while Operation Dynamo ran from 26 May to 4 June 1940. From here the defence of the perimeter was coordinated as 338,226 men were taken off to England, 239,555 of them from the harbour and 98,671 from the beaches. The museum inside tells the story of the battle and the evacuation, with weapons, uniforms, models, photographs and maps of the operations.

A long, low brick casemate set into the bastion wall on Rue des Chantiers de France. Inside, vaulted rooms hold uniforms, weapons, models of the ships and beaches, and maps of the shrinking pocket. Outside, a memorial to the French Navy of 1939–40 stands nearby.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.381271,51.046113,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Malo-les-Bains evacuation beach, Dunkirk</name><description>Tour day 8

The beaches running east from Malo-les-Bains towards Bray-Dunes and De Panne were where tens of thousands of British and French soldiers waited, often for days, under air attack. Small boats ferried men out to larger ships lying offshore, a slow process that led Captain William Tennant, the senior naval officer ashore, to send most troops to the harbour moles instead. In all 98,671 men were lifted from the beaches. At the western end of the promenade, the Digue des Alliés, the Allied memorial inaugurated in 1962 marks the annual commemoration of the battle and of Operation Dynamo.

A wide, flat sandy beach backed by the promenade and rebuilt seaside villas. At its western end stands a block-like memorial, inscribed in French only, to the soldiers, sailors and airmen of the French and Allied armies, with British poppy wreaths on its anchor. From here you can walk to the root of the East Mole.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.381005,51.048386,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dunkirk Memorial and Town Cemetery (CWGC)</name><description>Tour day 8

The Dunkirk Memorial commemorates more than 4,500 men of the British Expeditionary Force who died in the 1939–40 campaign, or later in captivity after being taken prisoner then, and have no known grave. Designed by Philip Hepworth, it was unveiled by Queen Elizabeth The Queen Mother on 29 June 1957. It forms the entrance to the British war graves section of Dunkirk Town Cemetery, on the road towards Veurne (Furnes) in Belgium, where men of both world wars are buried.

A colonnaded entrance with the names of the missing on its panels and an engraved glass panel by John Hutton showing the evacuation. Through it lie the Second World War plots (I and II) and First World War plots IV and V; the other First World War plots are reached via the civil cemetery entrance.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.389670,51.030600,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fort des Dunes, Leffrinckoucke</name><description>Tour day 8

Built in 1878–80 in the dunes east of Dunkirk, the fort was the headquarters of the French 12th Motorised Infantry Division, part of the rearguard holding the perimeter while the British and French embarked. On the evening of 2 June 1940 dive-bombers struck it and General Janssen, the division's commander, was mortally wounded in a courtyard with several officers. Further bombing on 3 June killed more men inside the fort. The French held long enough for the evacuation to run to 4 June. In September 1944 the Germans used the fort to shoot captured members of the Resistance against the north moat.

A sunken 19th-century fort with brick casemates, moats and courtyards, now a museum (the Musérial) on the 1940 battle and the evacuation. Plaques by the entrance remember the soldiers killed in 1940 and the executed resistance fighters. The national military cemetery lies next to the fort.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.444823,51.052833,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Zuydcoote and Bray-Dunes beach wrecks</name><description>Tour day 8

On 29 May 1940 the Thames paddle steamer HMS Crested Eagle, serving as an anti-aircraft ship, left the East Mole with about 600 men, including survivors of the bombed Grenade and Fenella. Bombs set her stern on fire less than a mile out and her captain, Lieutenant Commander Bernard Booth, ran her ashore opposite the Zuydcoote sanatorium; about 200 survivors were picked up. A few hundred metres away lies the Devonia, bombed on 30 May and beached to serve as a jetty. Further east at Bray-Dunes, soldiers drove lorries into the sea at low tide to build improvised piers.

At low tide, the blackened ribs and paddle-wheel frames of the Crested Eagle rise from the sand, with a plaque placed in 2015; the Devonia lies a few hundred metres east. On the Bray-Dunes seafront a monument honours the French 12th Motorised Infantry Division.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.491208,51.075821,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Plaine au Bois massacre memorial, Esquelbecq (Wormhout)</name><description>Tour day 9

On 28 May 1940 British units held Wormhout to cover the retreat to Dunkirk: the 2nd Royal Warwickshire Regiment, the 4th Cheshire Regiment and 210 Battery of the 53rd (Worcestershire Yeomanry) Anti-Tank Regiment. After they were overrun by the SS Leibstandarte Adolf Hitler, about 95 prisoners were herded into a cowshed at La Plaine au Bois and killed with grenades and gunfire. A handful survived, among them Privates Bert Evans and Arthur Johnson and Gunner Brian Fahey. Post-war testimony blamed Wilhelm Mohnke, who denied it and was never tried; he died in 2001.

A replica of the barn stands in the fields, reached by a path lined with 80 beech trees. Nearby are a stele inaugurated on 28 May 2010 to the Allied and civilian dead of Wormhout, Ledringhem and Esquelbecq, and an orientation table explaining the fighting. A separate memorial stands on the D17.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.442286,50.877818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Blockhaus d'Éperlecques</name><description>Tour day 9

Under the cover name Kraftwerk Nord West, the Organisation Todt began this bunker in the forest near Watten in March 1943 as a launch facility for V-2 rockets, planned for over 100 missiles and up to 36 launches a day. It was built largely by prisoners of war and conscripted workers held in nearby camps. On 27 August 1943, 187 US B-17s bombed it while the freshly poured concrete was still setting. Part was later finished as a liquid oxygen plant. Tallboy raids in June and July 1944, including on 6 July, caused severe damage, and construction stopped that month.

A colossal concrete block in the woods, its roof and walls scarred by bomb craters and the twisted reinforcing of the August 1943 raid. A woodland trail leads past V-weapons and a V-2 replica to the bunker, which you walk into and around.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.183610,50.828610,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Batterie Todt (Atlantic Wall Museum), Audinghen</name><description>Tour day 9

Work on the battery at Haringzelles, near Cap Gris-Nez, began on 22 July 1940. First called Siegfried, it was renamed after Fritz Todt, head of the Organisation Todt, who died in 1942. Four 380 mm naval guns in concrete casemates could reach the English coast, and the battery shelled shipping and the Dover area; the Cap Gris-Nez guns caused 3,059 alerts and 216 civilian deaths in England. On 29 September 1944, after heavy bombing, the North Nova Scotia Highlanders of the 3rd Canadian Infantry Division took it during Operation Undergo.

Casemate 1, a massive concrete gun house, holds the private Atlantic Wall Museum with uniforms, weapons and posters. Outside stands a 280 mm K5 railway gun, one of two survivors, brought here from Fort Nieulay at Calais in 1992. The other casemates lie in the surrounding fields.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>1.600035,50.843858,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Memorial of Civilians in Wartime, Falaise</name><description>Tour day 10

Falaise gave its name to the pocket in which the German armies were trapped in August 1944, and the town itself was largely destroyed by bombing and fighting before Canadian troops took it in mid-August. The Mémorial de Falaise stands on the site of a house destroyed in the 1944 bombing, in a building from the town's reconstruction, close to William the Conqueror's castle. Unlike most Normandy museums it tells the war from the point of view of civilians: occupation, shortages, bombing, evacuation and liberation, combining reconstructions made with the Caen Memorial and the memories of people who lived through it.

Reconstructed scenes of civilian life under occupation and bombing, with film and personal testimony. Afterwards walk up to the castle; the rebuilt town centre around it shows how much of Falaise had to be rebuilt.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.202343,48.894206,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Caen Memorial (Mémorial de Caen)</name><description>Tour day 11

Caen was a D-Day objective for the British, but it took until July to clear the city. Montgomery's offensives around it, including Epsom, Charnwood and Goodwood, drew in most of the German armour in Normandy. Bombing and shelling destroyed much of the city and killed many civilians. The Mémorial, opened by President Mitterrand on 6 June 1988, stands in the northern suburbs over a former German bunker. It is a large museum of the twentieth century, covering the road to war, the Second World War, the landings and Battle of Normandy, and the Cold War, with an emphasis on peace and reconciliation.

A large modern building with several permanent exhibitions and films; allow at least half a day. Outside are memorial gardens to the Americans, British and Canadians. Below, part of the German underground bunker can be visited.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.384135,49.197438,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Merville Battery</name><description>Tour day 12

The Allies believed this battery of four concrete casemates could fire on Sword Beach and the fleet. The 9th Parachute Battalion, 6th Airborne Division, under Lieutenant Colonel Terence Otway, was ordered to destroy it before dawn on 6 June. The drop was badly scattered and only about 150 of some 600 men reached the rendezvous, without most of their heavy equipment. Otway attacked anyway. The paratroopers broke in after a short, costly fight and put the guns out of action. They proved to be First World War-era 100 mm Czech howitzers, not the heavier guns that had been feared.

The four casemates survive in a wooded site with trenches and bunkers. Casemate No. 1 holds the museum, and another recreates the attack with sound and light. A C-47 Dakota, 'SNAFU Special', stands on the site, and a memorial to Otway is near the entrance.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.197690,49.270059,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sword Beach and the Grand Bunker, Ouistreham</name><description>Tour day 12

Sword was the easternmost beach, assaulted on 6 June by the British 3rd Infantry Division and No. 1 Special Service Brigade, including Commandant Philippe Kieffer's French commandos, who fought through Ouistreham. The 17-metre concrete tower behind the seafront was the fire-control post for the coastal batteries guarding the Orne estuary. Commandos bypassed it after being driven back on D-Day. On 9 June Lieutenant Bob Orrell of the Royal Engineers and three men blew in its armoured door, which took about four hours. Two officers and about fifty men surrendered to them.

The five-storey tower is restored as it was in June 1944: machine-room, dormitory, radio room and the rangefinder at the top, with views over the estuary. Sword Beach and the Kieffer commando memorial are a short walk away.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.252454,49.287525,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Longues-sur-Mer battery</name><description>Tour day 13

This naval battery, completed in early 1944 between Arromanches and Port-en-Bessin, had four 150 mm Škoda guns in concrete casemates and a fire-control post on the cliff edge. Its range of about 20 km covered both Gold and Omaha. On the morning of 6 June it fired on the fleet and duelled with the cruisers HMS Ajax and HMS Argonaut and the French cruisers Georges Leygues and Montcalm, which knocked out or damaged three casemates. On 7 June the garrison of about 180 men, many of them older soldiers, surrendered to C Company, 2nd Battalion, Devonshire Regiment.

Three casemates still hold their guns, the only battery in Normandy to keep them. A fourth gun is wrecked. Walk forward to the two-storey fire-control post on the cliff, which overlooks the sea and featured in the film The Longest Day.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.694502,49.343076,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Normandy American Cemetery, Colleville-sur-Mer</name><description>Tour day 13

On the plateau above Omaha Beach, the cemetery holds 9,389 American war dead, most killed in the landings and the Normandy campaign. The Walls of the Missing in the Garden of the Missing carry 1,557 names. It covers 172.5 acres and was dedicated on 19 July 1956. Among the graves are Brigadier General Theodore Roosevelt Jr., who landed with the first wave at Utah, buried beside his brother Quentin, killed in 1918, and two of the Niland brothers, whose story inspired Saving Private Ryan. The visitor centre was opened by the American Battle Monuments Commission in 2007.

Rows of white marble Latin crosses and Stars of David on lawns above the beach, a small chapel at the centre, and a colonnade with the bronze statue 'Spirit of American Youth Rising from the Waves'. The visitor centre explains the landings. An orientation table overlooks Omaha Beach.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.854756,49.359585,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Overlord Museum, Colleville-sur-Mer</name><description>Tour day 13

This private museum is built around the collection of Michel Leloup (1929–2011), who witnessed the fighting as a boy and later worked in Normandy's post-war reconstruction, gathering vehicles and equipment left on the battlefields. The collection was shown in Falaise from 1987 and moved to this purpose-built site near Omaha Beach in 2013. It covers the campaign from the landings to the liberation of Paris, with a short look at the earlier war, using large vehicle scenes that put German and Allied equipment side by side.

More than 35 vehicles, tanks and guns from the armies that fought in Normandy are shown in staged scenes, including Shermans, a Panther and a Panzer IV. Personal belongings of individual soldiers add the human scale.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.856375,49.347847,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Museum of the Battle of Normandy, Bayeux</name><description>Tour day 14

Bayeux was freed by the British 50th (Northumbrian) Division on 7 June 1944, almost undamaged, and was the first sizeable French town liberated. On 14 June General de Gaulle came ashore and spoke here, a step towards establishing his authority over liberated France. The museum, on the ring road named after Fabian Ware, founder of the Imperial War Graves Commission, covers the whole Battle of Normandy from June to August. It explains the campaign as a sequence, from the landings through the battles for Caen and the bocage to the Falaise Pocket.

Restored armoured vehicles, including a Sherman and a Churchill Crocodile, stand on the lawn outside. Inside are weapons, uniforms, vehicles, dioramas and an aviation room, with a film shown regularly. Bayeux War Cemetery is across the road.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.711559,49.273440,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bayeux War Cemetery and Memorial</name><description>Tour day 14

This is the largest Commonwealth Second World War cemetery in France, with 4,648 burials, mostly men killed in the Normandy campaign. More than 500 graves are of other nationalities, most of them German. It was begun in 1944, completed in 1952 and designed by Philip Hepworth for the Imperial (now Commonwealth) War Graves Commission. Across the road the Bayeux Memorial names more than 1,800 Commonwealth servicemen who died in the Battle of Normandy and have no known grave. Its Latin frieze reads, in translation: 'We, once conquered by William, have now set free the Conqueror's native land.'

Rows of Portland-stone headstones in lawns, with the Cross of Sacrifice and Stone of Remembrance. Across the road, the colonnaded Bayeux Memorial carries the names of the missing, and its Latin inscription about William the Conqueror runs across the front.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.714149,49.273986,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>La Cambe German war cemetery</name><description>Tour day 14

La Cambe holds 21,222 German servicemen, the largest German war cemetery in Normandy. It began on 10 June 1944 as a battlefield cemetery laid out by the US 607th Quartermaster Graves Registration Company, with American and German dead in neighbouring fields. After the war the American dead were repatriated or moved to Colleville, and Germans were gathered here from about 1,400 field graves across Normandy. The Volksbund, the German war graves commission, maintains it. Among the dead is Tiger commander Michael Wittmann, killed with his crew on 8 August 1944 and found in 1983.

Flat stone grave markers, each usually for two men, lie among groups of dark basalt crosses. A grassy mound in the centre, topped by a large cross and figures, covers a mass grave of 207 unknown and 89 identified soldiers. A peace garden of maple trees and a small information centre are by the entrance.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.026390,49.341940,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pointe du Hoc</name><description>Tour day 14

German guns on this 35-metre headland between Utah and Omaha were thought to threaten both beaches. On 6 June 1944, 225 men of the 2nd Ranger Battalion under Lieutenant Colonel James Earl Rudder landed late, at about 07:10, and climbed the cliffs with rocket-fired grapnels and ladders under fire. At the top they found the casemates empty: the guns had been moved inland. Patrols found five of the six in an orchard and wrecked them with thermite grenades by about 09:00. The Rangers then held the headland against counter-attacks until relieved on 8 June, by when only about 90 were still fit to fight.

The ground is still a moonscape of bomb craters around ruined casemates and the observation bunker at the cliff edge, topped by the granite Ranger Monument. Look over the edge at the cliffs the Rangers climbed.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.989361,49.397382,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Utah Beach Museum, Sainte-Marie-du-Mont</name><description>Tour day 15

Utah was the westernmost beach, added to the plan so that Cherbourg could be taken quickly. On 6 June 1944 the US 4th Infantry Division landed here; currents carried the first waves well south of their planned sector, onto a stretch that turned out to be less heavily defended. Brigadier General Theodore Roosevelt Jr., with the first wave, decided to bring the follow-up forces in where they had landed. The division got ashore with far lighter losses than at Omaha and linked up with the airborne troops inland. The museum stands on the beach, on the site of a German strongpoint.

Displays, models and many vehicles inside and outside, and a hangar housing a B-26 Marauder bomber. Large windows look out on the beach. Outside are monuments, a Sherman tank and the dunes the 4th Division crossed.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.174668,49.415325,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sainte-Mère-Église and the Airborne Museum</name><description>Tour day 15

Sainte-Mère-Église sat astride the main road to Cherbourg behind Utah Beach. In the early hours of 6 June 1944 paratroopers of the US 82nd Airborne Division dropped on and around the town; some landed in the square, lit by a burning house, and were shot as they came down. Men of the 505th Parachute Infantry Regiment took the town before dawn, making it one of the first places in France liberated on D-Day. Paratrooper John Steele was left hanging from the church tower by his parachute. The Airborne Museum, opened in 1964, tells the story of both American airborne divisions.

In the square, a dummy paratrooper hangs from the church tower, and inside the church stained-glass windows show paratroopers. The Airborne Museum has a real Waco glider and a C-47 that flew in the 5–6 June drops, plus the walk-through 'Operation Neptune' building.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.314964,49.408249,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Brest: the U-boat base and the Naval Museum</name><description>Tour day 16

German troops entered Brest on 19 June 1940. From 1941 the Organisation Todt built a U-boat base of 15 basins, its roof thickened to 6.1 m, for the 1st and 9th U-boat Flotillas. It survived 82 raids; Tallboys from 617 Squadron pierced the roof but did little damage inside. After D-Day the city became Festung Brest under General Hermann-Bernhard Ramcke. US VIII Corps besieged it from 7 August to 19 September 1944, taking about 38,000 prisoners; the old city was razed and the port wrecked. On 9 September an explosion in the Sadi-Carnot shelter killed 371 French civilians.

The medieval château, still the seat of the Maritime Prefecture, houses the National Naval Museum, with ship models, paintings and the harbour's naval history. Its ramparts look over the military port; the U-boat base lies inside the naval zone and cannot be visited. The Sadi-Carnot shelter, with its memorial, is in the city centre.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-4.492839,48.381800,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Keroman submarine base, Lorient</name><description>Tour day 17

On 7 July 1940 Fritz-Julius Lemp's U-30 became the first U-boat to refuel at a French base, at Lorient. From November 1940 to March 1942 Admiral Dönitz directed the Atlantic campaign from a villa at Kernevel, facing the harbour. The Kriegsmarine built three huge pen blocks at Keroman from 1941; the largest, K3, could shelter 13 submarines under a roof over seven metres thick. The 2nd and 10th U-boat Flotillas were based here. Raids from January to February 1943 flattened nearly 90% of Lorient but left the base intact. The garrison held out until May 1945.

The grey bulk of K1, K2 and K3 dominates the peninsula. Guided tours go inside K3. Nearby you can board the French submarine Flore (S645) and visit a 1942 bunker used to train submariners in escape, now the Musée sous-marin, as well as the Cité de la Voile.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-3.366130,47.727421,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Saint-Nazaire: U-boat base and Normandie dock</name><description>Tour day 17

Saint-Nazaire's 14-pen U-boat base, built from February 1941 to mid-1942, served the 6th and 7th Flotillas. Beside it lay the Normandie dry dock, the only Atlantic dock that could take the battleship Tirpitz. In Operation Chariot on 28 March 1942, the explosive-packed destroyer HMS Campbeltown, under Lieutenant Commander Stephen Beattie, rammed the dock gate at 01:34 while commandos of No. 2 Commando under Lieutenant Colonel Charles Newman wrecked installations. Campbeltown blew up around noon, and the dock was out of use until 1948. Of 612 men, 169 were killed and 215 captured; five Victoria Crosses were awarded.

The vast concrete base, with a fortified lock of 1943–44 and a roof-top viewing terrace, now holds the Escal'Atlantic liner exhibition and the submarine Espadon. The Normandie dock (Forme Joubert) is close by, and a stele to the Chariot raiders stands in Petit-Maroc facing the Loire.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-2.202500,47.275000,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Oradour-sur-Glane: ruined village and Centre de la mémoire</name><description>Tour day 18

Four days after D-Day, the 1st Battalion of the SS Panzergrenadier Regiment 'Der Führer', part of the 2nd SS Panzer Division Das Reich, surrounded Oradour-sur-Glane. Its commander, Adolf Diekmann, acted after a fellow officer, Helmut Kämpfe, had been seized by the Resistance. The men were shot in barns and sheds, which were set alight; the women and children were locked in the church, which was set on fire. 642 people were killed. Marguerite Rouffanche escaped through a church window. In 1953 a Bordeaux military tribunal convicted 19 of 20 defendants, but an amnesty freed the Alsatian conscripts among them, causing lasting bitterness in the Limousin.

The ruined village has been left as it was: burnt-out houses, the church, rusting cars, sewing machines and bicycles, tramlines along the main street, and plaques naming families. The Centre de la mémoire, at the entrance between the old and new villages, displays objects recovered from the ruins.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>1.040388,45.929570,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Musée de la Libération de Paris – Leclerc – Jean Moulin</name><description>Tour day 19

The museum opened on 25 August 2019, the 75th anniversary of the Liberation, in the 18th-century Ledoux pavilions on Place Denfert-Rochereau. It brings together collections on General Philippe Leclerc, whose French 2nd Armoured Division entered Paris on 24–25 August 1944, and on Jean Moulin, de Gaulle's envoy who unified the Resistance and died after his arrest in 1943. Beneath the building is the underground command post of Colonel Henri Rol-Tanguy, commander of the Paris-region FFI, from which the uprising of 19–25 August 1944 was directed.

Exhibition rooms on occupation, resistance and liberation, with Leclerc and Moulin material, then a descent of about a hundred steps to Rol-Tanguy's command post, a concrete civil-defence shelter with its original telephones and fittings.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.331800,48.834110,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mémorial de la Shoah, Paris</name><description>Tour day 19

Around 76,000 Jews were deported from France between 1942 and 1944, the great majority to Auschwitz, with the help of the Vichy authorities and French police; few returned. The Mémorial de la Shoah in the Marais, formerly the Memorial of the Unknown Jewish Martyr, reopened in its present form on 27 January 2005, inaugurated by President Jacques Chirac. It combines a museum, archive and memorial. The crypt holds ashes from the death camps and the Warsaw Ghetto, buried in 1957 in soil from Israel.

In the forecourt, the Wall of Names lists about 76,000 deported Jews by year of deportation. Outside, the Wall of the Righteous honours those recognised by Yad Vashem for saving Jews in France. Inside are the crypt, the permanent exhibition and a room of photographs of deported children.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.356196,48.854846,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mont-Valérien memorial, Suresnes</name><description>Tour day 19

Between 1941 and 1944 the German army shot 1,008 men at Fort Mont-Valérien, a 19th-century fort above the Seine west of Paris; most were resistance fighters and hostages. Prisoners waited in a disused chapel and were taken to a clearing about 100 metres away to be shot. Among them were the naval officer Honoré d'Estienne d'Orves on 29 August 1941, the communist deputy Gabriel Péri on 15 December 1941, and Missak Manouchian and members of his FTP-MOI group, the 'Affiche Rouge', on 21 February 1944. De Gaulle consecrated the site as a memorial on 18 June 1945.

The Memorial of Fighting France, a long wall with a large Cross of Lorraine, stands below the fort. A path leads up to the chapel, with graffiti left by the condemned, and to the execution clearing, where a monument records the names of those shot.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>2.213830,48.871600,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fort de Schoenenbourg (Maginot Line)</name><description>Tour day 21

Schoenenbourg is a gros ouvrage of the Maginot Line, with six combat blocks and two entrances linked by deep tunnels, manned in 1939 by 491 men and 17 officers under Commandant Reynier. In June 1940, with the line outflanked, it held out alone: Stukas attacked on 19, 20 and 21 June, and from 21 June 420 mm siege mortars shelled it for three days. Its walls cracked but it kept firing; over the campaign it fired more than 17,000 shells and took over 3,000 shells and 160 bombs. Undefeated, it surrendered on 1 July 1940 under the armistice.

A walk of several kilometres through tunnels 30 metres underground, past barracks, kitchens, the power station and magazines, to the combat blocks and a retractable gun turret. Above ground, the craters of 1940 are still visible.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>7.911940,48.966670,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Luxembourg American Cemetery, Hamm</name><description>Tour day 23

The 609th Quartermaster Company of the US Third Army opened a temporary cemetery here at Hamm on 29 December 1944, while the Battle of the Bulge was still being fought. Dedicated in 1960, the cemetery covers about 20 hectares and holds 5,076 American dead, most of whom died in Luxembourg between September 1944 and February 1945. Among them are 101 unknowns, 22 pairs of brothers, 118 Jewish soldiers under Star of David headstones and an army nurse. General George S. Patton, commander of the Third Army, who died in December 1945, is buried at the head of the graves between the two flagpoles.

Pass the white stone chapel with its stained-glass window of five US command insignia and mosaic ceiling. Headstones fan out across the lawn; Patton's grave stands alone at the front between the flagpoles, facing his men.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>6.185560,49.611670,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>National Museum of Military History, Diekirch</name><description>Tour day 23

Diekirch, on the Sauer in north-eastern Luxembourg, lay on the southern flank of the Bulge. On the night of 18 January 1945 units of the US 5th Infantry Division crossed the river here as the Americans went over to the offensive. The museum grew out of the Diekirch Historical Museum, opened in 1984 in part of the former brewery, and now fills about 3,200 m² over four floors. Its life-size dioramas, centred on the night crossing of the Sauer, are surrounded by weapons, uniforms, photographs and more than 50 vehicles and guns from both sides. A second section covers the Luxembourg Army from 1815 to its UN missions.

Start with the large diorama of the night-time Sauer crossing, then work through four floors of dioramas, vehicles and personal belongings from both sides. Look for the working Enigma cipher machine. The museum is in the old brewery buildings about 200 m from the town centre.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>6.159048,49.871023,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mardasson Memorial, Bastogne</name><description>Tour day 24

The memorial stands on Mardasson hill, chosen partly because it lies on the divide between the Meuse and Rhine basins and looks over the ground held during the siege. Earth from the site was presented to President Truman on 4 July 1946. Designed by the Belgian architect Georges Dedoyard, the memorial was inaugurated on 16 July 1950 in the presence of Prince Baudouin and General Anthony McAuliffe. It is a five-pointed star 12 m high with 31 m sides, carrying the names of the then 48 US states and, on its pillars, the history of the battle and the main American units. In 2020 the Belgian state handed its management to the American Battle Monuments Commission.

Climb the spiral stairs to the walkway on top for a panorama of the siege perimeter. Read the ten stone panels describing the battle and the Latin dedication from the Belgian people, then go down to the crypt with three altars and mosaics by Fernand Léger.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.739270,50.009330,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bastogne Barracks (McAuliffe's HQ)</name><description>Tour day 24

In December 1944 the Heintz Barracks on the La Roche road housed the headquarters of the 101st Airborne Division. With its commander, Maxwell Taylor, away, the division was led by Brigadier General Anthony McAuliffe. On 22 December General Heinrich von Lüttwitz of XLVII Panzer Corps sent a written demand for surrender; McAuliffe's reply was 'NUTS!'. Bad weather prevented air resupply until 23 December, and Patton's Third Army reached the town from Assenois on 26 December. The barracks remained a Belgian Army site and since 2010 has been an interpretation centre, now run by the War Heritage Institute, with a workshop restoring armoured vehicles from the national collection.

Visits take you to the 'Nuts' cellar, where McAuliffe's headquarters is reconstructed, then through renovated halls of Second World War vehicles and equipment. You may see restorers at work in the vehicle workshop and, on some days, engines being run.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.718910,50.009730,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bois Jacques foxholes, Foy</name><description>Tour day 24

During the siege, Easy Company, 2nd Battalion, 506th Parachute Infantry Regiment, 101st Airborne Division, held foxholes in the Bois Jacques as part of the Bastogne perimeter, facing German-held Foy. The men endured snow, shortages and German tree bursts that showered splinters and shrapnel into their holes. On 24 December about 45 Germans attacked the position and were beaten off, for one American casualty. On 13 January 1945 the company attacked out of the wood to retake Foy. Their story became widely known through Stephen E. Ambrose's book Band of Brothers and the television series. A memorial on the Bizory–Foy road honours the company and 14 of its men killed in the area.

From the Easy Company memorial on the Bizory–Foy road, paths lead into the managed pine wood where the hollows of foxholes are still visible. Stay on the paths and leave anything you find. Look out across the open ground towards Foy, which the company attacked in January 1945.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.756707,50.028903,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Recogne German war cemetery</name><description>Tour day 24

The US Army opened a cemetery at Recogne, north of Bastogne, on 4 February 1945, burying about 2,700 Americans and over 3,000 Germans there. The American dead were later moved to Henri-Chapelle or repatriated, and the Belgian authorities concentrated German graves from field burials and local cemeteries here or at Lommel. Recogne now holds 6,807 German dead, most killed around Bastogne in the Battle of the Bulge, together with men who died in 1940 or during the occupation. The cemetery passed to the German War Graves Commission (Volksbund) in the 1950s and reopened to the public in 1960 after refurbishment.

Beyond the entrance building, rows of low grey stone crosses stand on lawn among trees. Compare its subdued atmosphere with the white headstones and open lawns of the American cemeteries at Henri-Chapelle and Hamm.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.740871,50.048420,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Houffalize and its Panther tank</name><description>Tour day 25

Houffalize, on the Ourthe north of Bastogne, was taken by the 116th Panzer Division on 19 December 1944 and became a crossroads for German supply columns. On the night of 5–6 January 1945 about ninety RAF Lancasters flattened the town to block it. The Germans withdrew on 15 January, and on 16 January the US First Army, attacking from the north, and the Third Army from the south met at Houffalize, closing the Bulge. The Panther now on display, originally numbered 111, had fought in Normandy, the Netherlands and the Hürtgen Forest. It overturned into the Ourthe from a bridge, killing its five crew, was pulled out in 1948 and restored in 2023.

The restored Panther Ausf. G stands on Square Saint-Pair by the Bastogne road, with about a dozen information panels and a small exhibition in a reconstructed period barracks. Walk to the Ourthe bridge on the road to La Roche, where the tank fell.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.790701,50.128941,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>St Vith</name><description>Tour day 25

St Vith, a German-speaking town transferred to Belgium in 1925, controlled a road junction the German plan expected to take by 18:00 on 17 December 1944. Instead Brigadier General Bruce Clarke's Combat Command B of the 7th Armored Division, with the 424th Infantry of the 106th Division, CCB of the 9th Armored Division and the 112th Infantry, held the approaches until 21 December, wrecking the German timetable. Meanwhile the 106th's 422nd and 423rd Infantry, cut off in the Schnee Eifel, surrendered on 19 December, about 7,000 men. American and RAF bombers destroyed the town on 25–26 December, and American forces retook it on 23 January 1945.

Almost nothing pre-war survived except the Büchel Tower. Look for the 106th Infantry Division monument on Klosterstrasse, the 2nd Infantry Division memorial and the plaques in the park on Pulverstrasse. The Prümerberg, just east of the town, was the last defensive line.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>6.128386,50.272141,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baugnez: Malmedy massacre memorial</name><description>Tour day 25

On 17 December 1944 the vanguard of Kampfgruppe Peiper met a convoy of Battery B, 285th Field Artillery Observation Battalion, at the Baugnez crossroads south-east of Malmedy. After a brief fight the Americans surrendered and were assembled in a field beside the road, where they were machine-gunned; many were then shot in the head. Eighty-four prisoners died. Some feigned death or ran, and 43 survivors reached American-held Malmedy, spreading word of the killings. At the Dachau trials in 1946, 73 men were convicted and 43 sentenced to death, Peiper among them. No death sentence was carried out, and Peiper was paroled in December 1956.

A low stone wall at the junction carries a plaque explaining the events and stones naming the dead. The field where the prisoners were assembled and shot lies beside the road. Across the junction stands the building of the former Baugnez 44 museum.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>6.066738,50.403689,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>December 44 Historical Museum, La Gleize</name><description>Tour day 25

Kampfgruppe Peiper, the armoured spearhead drawn from the 1st SS Panzer Division, drove west from 16 December 1944 towards the Meuse. Without supplies or contact with other German units, it was stopped in the Amblève valley. American attacks at Stoumont forced its remnants back to La Gleize, where they were almost trapped; on 21 December they captured Hal McCown, commander of 2nd Battalion, 119th Infantry. Short of fuel and with heavy losses, Joachim Peiper abandoned his vehicles on 24 December and led his remaining men out on foot, leaving wounded and prisoners behind. By his own account 717 men got back out of 3,000. The village museum, renovated in 2013, is devoted to these events.

On the square stands Tiger II number 213, abandoned in December 1944, its broken gun barrel repaired in 1972. Inside the museum are 17 dioramas, vehicles and guns, Peiper's map case and many objects found around La Gleize. The church opposite was badly damaged in 1944 and rebuilt.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.845590,50.410059,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Henri-Chapelle American Cemetery</name><description>Tour day 26

Troops of the US 1st Infantry Division liberated this area on 12 September 1944, and a temporary cemetery opened a few hundred metres to the north on 28 September. The permanent cemetery, dedicated in 1960, covers 57 acres on a ridge near Hombourg. It holds 7,992 American dead, 94 of them unknown, most killed in the Battle of the Bulge or the advance into Germany in 1944–45, together with airmen lost over the region. A colonnade lists 450 missing; bronze rosettes mark those since found and identified. In 1947 the first repatriation of American war dead to the United States began here.

Headstones curve across a sloping lawn facing the memorial, with a road running through the site. The chapel and map room show carved granite maps of the American advance, the colonnade carries the names of the missing, and the Angel of Peace statue stands nearby.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.899079,50.696607,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fort Eben-Emael</name><description>Tour day 26

Built in 1931–35 above the Caestert cutting of the Albert Canal, Eben-Emael was the largest fort of the Fortified Position of Liège, armed with 120 mm, 75 mm and 60 mm guns in turrets and casemates and linked by over 4 km of galleries. At dawn on 10 May 1940 Assault Group Granit of Sturmabteilung Koch, 85 men in 11 DFS 230 gliders, set out for its roof. Nine landed; Oberleutnant Rudolf Witzig's tow rope snapped, so Oberfeldwebel Helmut Wenzel took charge. Shaped charges and a flamethrower knocked out guns and cupolas while other groups seized the Veldwezelt and Vroenhoven bridges. The garrison surrendered at 12:30 on 11 May; Belgian losses were 23 killed and 59 wounded, Granit's 6 killed.

The fort is still Belgian Army property. Inside are the underground barracks, a museum, a 180° film of the attack and long, cold galleries. Above ground you can walk the grassed roof between the blocks, see casemates and cupolas holed by German charges, and look down into the canal cutting.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.673331,50.796800,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Netherlands American Cemetery, Margraten</name><description>Tour day 26

The only American war cemetery in the Netherlands lies beside the old Roman road east of Maastricht, in the first part of the country to be liberated, in September 1944. It holds more than 8,000 American dead, many from the US Ninth Army's fighting in the Rhineland and the advance into Germany, as well as from Market Garden and the air war. The Tablets of the Missing list 1,722 names. Since 1945 local families have adopted graves and names, visiting and laying flowers; today every grave has an adopter, and there is a waiting list. The cemetery was dedicated in 1960 and opened by Queen Juliana.

A long approach through a court of honour with the Tablets of the Missing, a tall memorial tower and chapel, and beyond them sixteen plots of white marble crosses and Stars of David on a broad lawn. A visitor building at the entrance tells the story.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.807900,50.817620,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Overloon War Museum</name><description>Tour day 28

After Market Garden the Germans held a bridgehead west of the Maas around Venlo. From 30 September 1944 the US 7th Armored Division attacked Overloon and failed, losing many tanks in the woods. The British 3rd Infantry Division, supported by 11th Armoured Division, took over and captured Overloon and then Venray by 18 October; the 1st Royal Norfolks suffered especially heavy losses. The village was destroyed. So much wrecked equipment was left on the battlefield that a museum was set up among it, opening on 25 May 1946 as the National War and Resistance Museum, the oldest Second World War museum in Western Europe.

Museum halls set in the wooded park where the battle was fought. Highlights include a Panther tank knocked out at Overloon, a running T-34, a B-25 Mitchell, a Spitfire, a Horsa glider, a Dakota, the 'Turning Point Europe' exhibition and a Lancaster pavilion. Allow several hours.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.956940,51.570560,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Freedom Museum (Vrijheidsmuseum), Groesbeek</name><description>Tour day 28

Groesbeek stands on the wooded heights south-east of Nijmegen, where the US 82nd Airborne Division landed on 17 September 1944 to cover the Nijmegen bridges from German attack out of the Reichswald. For five months the village was a front-line town. In February 1945 Operation Veritable, the Anglo-Canadian offensive into the Rhineland, was launched from here. The Freedom Museum, formerly the National Liberation Museum 1944–1945, tells the story of the Netherlands from the 1930s through occupation and liberation to post-war rebuilding, and its Honorary Dome lists the names of Allied soldiers who died between D-Day and 8 May 1945.

A modern museum building on the Wylerbaan. Look for the Waco glider nose section, the large model of Operation Market Garden, a hiding-place cupboard where visitors can listen to Radio Orange, an air-raid shelter and the Honorary Dome of names.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.937005,51.785650,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Groesbeek Canadian War Cemetery and Memorial</name><description>Tour day 28

Most of the men buried here died in February and March 1945 in Operation Veritable and the Rhineland battles fought out of the Groesbeek area, and the cemetery was sited so the Canadian dead lay close to the German soil where they fell. It holds 2,619 Commonwealth graves, 2,338 of them Canadian and 268 British. In the shelters stands the Groesbeek Memorial to more than 1,000 Commonwealth soldiers with no known grave who died in north-west Europe from late August 1944 to the end of the war, among them the Victoria Cross holders Sgt Aubrey Cosens and L/Sgt John Baskeyfield, killed at Oosterbeek.

A large, quiet CWGC cemetery on a hill, with terraced rows of headstones, the Cross of Sacrifice and the colonnaded Groesbeek Memorial carrying panels of names. On clear days there are views towards the Reichswald and the German border.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.931866,51.798346,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Waal crossing memorial (De Oversteek), Nijmegen</name><description>Tour day 28

With German troops holding both Nijmegen road and rail bridges, General James Gavin ordered Major Julian Cook's 3rd Battalion, 504th Parachute Infantry Regiment (82nd Airborne Division), to cross the Waal by boat and take the north ends. Only 26 canvas assault boats arrived. At 15:00 on 20 September 1944 the paratroopers paddled across the 250-metre-wide river about two kilometres downstream of the road bridge, near the Gelderland power station, under machine-gun, mortar and artillery fire. Survivors stormed the dyke and by evening had taken the north end of the railway bridge; the road bridge also fell, and British tanks of the Guards Armoured Division crossed that evening.

The 2013 bridge 'De Oversteek' spans the river where the boats crossed. A plaque on the Generaal Gavinsingel in Lent marks the landing. Each evening at sunset a veteran-led 'Sunset March' crosses the bridge as its 48 pairs of lights come on one by one, one for each man killed.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.842522,51.859977,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Airborne Museum Hartenstein, Oosterbeek</name><description>Tour day 29

Villa Hartenstein, a hotel since 1942, became the headquarters of Major-General Roy Urquhart's British 1st Airborne Division in September 1944. From 21 September it stood at the centre of the Oosterbeek perimeter, about three miles round, held by some 3,600 men under constant shelling. Polish paratroopers dropped near Driel on 21 September but few could cross the Rhine. On the night of 25/26 September (Operation Berlin) about 2,400 men were ferried back across the river. The division lost nearly three-quarters of its strength. Urquhart himself opened the museum in the restored villa on 11 May 1978.

The white villa, scarred and restored, with a British 17-pounder anti-tank gun on the lawn. Inside are weapons, uniforms and personal stories of British, Polish and German soldiers and civilians. Below ground the 'Airborne Experience' takes you through a glider fuselage and into reconstructed street fighting.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.832667,51.987748,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Old Church and Kate ter Horst house, Oosterbeek</name><description>Tour day 29

The Old Church (Oude Kerk) on the Benedendorpsweg stood at the south-eastern corner of the Oosterbeek perimeter, held by the mixed 'Lonsdale Force' of survivors from the battalions that had failed to reach the bridge. Next door, the old rectory of Jan and Kate ter Horst became a regimental aid post. Over eight days some 250 wounded men lay in its rooms while the house was repeatedly hit. Kate ter Horst, a mother of five, helped the doctors, sat with the dying and in the evenings read psalms to the wounded from an English Bible; the soldiers called her 'the Angel of Arnhem'. The church was left in ruins and reopened in 1949.

A small restored church with a memorial stone by the entrance ending 'Not one shall be forgotten', and nearby a bench and Battle Marker for Lonsdale Force. The ter Horst house next door is a private home: view it from the road only. The Rhine meadows where the evacuation took place lie just south.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.838030,51.978183,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Arnhem Oosterbeek War Cemetery</name><description>Tour day 29

The cemetery gathers the dead of the Battle of Arnhem from temporary graves across the area. It holds around 1,760 Second World War graves, more than 240 of them unidentified, mostly British but also Polish, Canadian and Dutch. Among them are three posthumous Victoria Cross recipients: Lt John Grayburn of the 2nd Parachute Battalion, who fought at the bridge; Capt Lionel Queripel of the 10th Battalion; and Flt Lt David Lord of 271 Squadron RAF, who kept his burning Dakota over the drop zone to deliver supplies. Since 1945 local schoolchildren have laid flowers on every grave at the annual commemoration in September.

A CWGC cemetery of white Portland headstones on a lawn edged by woodland, with the Cross of Sacrifice and Stone of Remembrance. Look for the Polish graves with their distinct pointed headstones, and the VC graves of Grayburn, Queripel and Lord.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.848330,51.993060,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ginkel Heath drop zone, Ede</name><description>Tour day 29

Ginkel Heath, west of Arnhem, was Drop Zone Y. On 17 September 1944 the 7th Battalion King's Own Scottish Borderers landed nearby and spent the night holding the heath against growing German pressure. Brigadier John Hackett's 4th Parachute Brigade was due on the second lift, but ground fog in England delayed it by several hours. When the brigade jumped after 15:00 on 18 September it came down under fire on a contested drop zone, and lost men and time before it could move off towards Arnhem. The delay of the second lift was one of the reasons the division could not reinforce the bridge.

A wide, open heath of heather and sand, still much as it was in 1944. The airborne memorial stands beside the N224 (Verlengde Arnhemseweg) on the edge of the heath. Stand by it and look across the open ground the paratroopers had to cross under fire.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.734048,52.036944,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Grebbeberg battlefield and military cemetery</name><description>Tour day 30

The Grebbeberg, a wooded hill above the Rhine east of Rhenen, anchored the southern end of the Grebbe Line, the main Dutch defence position in 1940. From 11 to 13 May the German 207th Infantry Division and the SS regiment 'Der Führer' attacked troops of the Dutch II Corps, including the 8th Infantry Regiment. The Dutch held for three days, but a counter-attack on 13 May failed and the line was abandoned that night, the army falling back to the Water Line. The military cemetery (Militair Ereveld) was laid out on the hill on 20 May 1940; the German dead once buried here were later moved to Ysselsteyn.

Rows of white headstones on a slope under beech woods, a Cross of Remembrance at the entrance, regimental monuments to the 8th and 22nd Infantry, and J.J.P. Oud's national army monument. In the woods behind, Dutch casemates and trench lines of the Grebbe Line survive.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>5.600280,51.955830,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Anne Frank House, Amsterdam</name><description>Tour day 30

On 6 July 1942 Otto Frank's family went into hiding in the Secret Annex behind his business at Prinsengracht 263. The van Pels family followed a week later and Fritz Pfeffer in November: eight people in all, supplied by helpers including Miep Gies, Bep Voskuijl, Johannes Kleiman and Victor Kugler. On 4 August 1944 police raided the annex and arrested them. They were sent via Westerbork to Auschwitz; Anne and Margot died of typhus at Bergen-Belsen in February 1945. Only Otto survived. Anne's diary, saved by Miep Gies, was published as 'Het Achterhuis' on 25 June 1947. The house opened as a museum on 3 May 1960.

A narrow canal-side house. Behind the moveable bookcase are the empty rooms of the Secret Annex, left unfurnished at Otto Frank's wish, with Anne's film-star pictures still on her wall and the pencil marks of the children's heights. The original diary is displayed.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>4.884320,52.375250,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Camp Westerbork Memorial Centre</name><description>Tour day 31

Westerbork was built by the Dutch government in 1939 to house Jewish refugees from Germany. From 1 July 1942 the Germans used it as the transit camp for the deportation of Jews, and also Sinti and Roma, from the Netherlands. Ninety-three trains left Westerbork: 65 to Auschwitz, 19 to Sobibór and 9 to Theresienstadt and Bergen-Belsen. Anne Frank and the others from the Secret Annex arrived on 8 August 1944 and were put on the last train to Auschwitz on 3 September. Canadian troops liberated the camp on 12 April 1945, finding 876 prisoners. The memorial centre opened in 1983.

The museum tells individual stories through film, letters and objects, including footage shot in the camp in 1944. A shuttle bus runs 3 km to the camp site, where the 102,000 stones memorial, the National Monument of curled rails and a goods wagon stand among the outlines of the barracks.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>6.569700,52.921080,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bergen-Belsen Memorial</name><description>Tour day 31

Bergen-Belsen began in 1940 as a Wehrmacht camp for prisoners of war; almost 20,000 Soviet prisoners died there. From 1943 the SS used part of it as an exchange camp for Jews, but in the last months it became a dumping ground for prisoners evacuated from other camps. Typhus spread through the overcrowded huts; Anne and Margot Frank died there in February or March 1945. On 15 April 1945 British troops, including 11th Armoured Division, found about 60,000 prisoners and more than 13,000 unburied bodies. Around 14,000 more died after liberation. The British burned the huts, and BBC reporter Richard Dimbleby's broadcast shocked Britain.

Nothing of the camp stands. A documentation centre opened in 2007 leads onto open heath with grassed mass graves marked by stones giving numbers buried, an obelisk and inscription wall, the Jewish monument and memorial stones, including one for Anne and Margot Frank.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>9.911680,52.759440,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ravensbrück Memorial</name><description>Tour day 33

Ravensbrück, 90 km north of Berlin, opened in May 1939 when the SS moved 900 women there from Lichtenburg. It was the only large concentration camp built for women, and an estimated 130,000 to 132,000 passed through it, from across occupied Europe. Prisoners worked in SS textile workshops and for firms such as Siemens. From summer 1942 SS doctors carried out experiments on 86 women, mostly Polish, whom they called 'rabbits'. In January 1945 the SS converted a hut near the crematorium into a gas chamber. The Red Army liberated the camp on 30 April 1945, finding fewer than 3,500 prisoners alive.

On the Schwedtsee shore Will Lammert's bronze 'Tragende', a woman carrying another, faces Fürstenberg across the lake. See the crematorium, the camp road, the female guards' houses and the main exhibition in the former commandant's building.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.170780,53.189580,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sachsenhausen Memorial and Museum</name><description>Tour day 33

Built in July 1936 just north of Berlin, Sachsenhausen was laid out as a model camp, a triangle overseen from a single gatehouse, and it trained SS camp staff. More than 200,000 people passed through, including Soviet prisoners of war murdered in large numbers in 1941. Station Z, with its crematoria and later a gas chamber, was built outside the wall in 1942, and prisoners forged British and American banknotes for Operation Bernhard. In April 1945 the SS drove most prisoners out on a death march; Soviet and Polish troops freed about 3,400 left behind on 22 April. The NKVD then used the camp as Special Camp No. 7 until 1950.

Enter through Tower A, with its 'Arbeit macht frei' gate, onto the semicircular roll-call ground. See the reconstructed barracks, the cell block, the foundations of Station Z and the 1961 East German obelisk. Exhibitions cover the camp, the Soviet special camp and the memorial.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.264170,52.765830,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Soviet War Memorial, Tiergarten</name><description>Tour day 34

Built within months of the fighting and unveiled on 11 November 1945, this was the first Soviet memorial in Berlin. Architect Mikhail Gorvits designed the curved colonnade, and Lev Kerbel and Vladimir Tsigal sculpted the bronze soldier on top. More than 2,000 of the Red Army soldiers killed in the Battle of Berlin are buried behind it. Two T-34 tanks and two ML-20 152mm guns, said to be among the first to enter the city, flank the approach. The memorial stood in the British sector, so throughout the Cold War Soviet guards marched into West Berlin to mount guard here under British protection.

A curved colonnade of reddish stone topped by a soldier with a rifle slung, on Straße des 17. Juni a few hundred metres west of the Brandenburg Gate. Look for the tanks and guns on their plinths and the inscriptions in Russian.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.372220,52.516670,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe</name><description>Tour day 34

Germany's central Holocaust memorial was designed by the American architect Peter Eisenman and dedicated on 10 May 2005, sixty years after the war ended. It covers a whole city block between the Brandenburg Gate and Potsdamer Platz, close to where Hitler's Chancellery stood. The field of 2,711 concrete stelae on undulating ground carries no names or symbols; Eisenman meant it to unsettle rather than explain. The explanation lies underneath, in the Information Centre, which tells the stories of individual families and holds the names of about three million Jewish victims obtained from Yad Vashem.

Walk into the field: the ground dips and the stelae rise above head height, cutting off the city. The entrance to the underground Information Centre is at the south-east corner, with rooms on individual victims, families and the sites of murder across Europe.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.378610,52.513890,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Site of the Führerbunker</name><description>Tour day 34

Hitler moved into the bunker beneath the Reich Chancellery garden in January 1945 and directed the last months of the war from there. The main bunker's floor lay 8.5 metres underground. On 29 April he married Eva Braun, and on 30 April 1945 both killed themselves in his study; their bodies were burned in the garden above. Soviet troops reached the bunker on 2 May. The Soviets and later the East German authorities demolished the Chancellery and most of the bunker, and flats and a car park were built over the site in the late 1980s. Nothing of it is visible.

A car park and blocks of flats at the corner of Gertrud-Kolmar-Straße and In den Ministergärten. An information board put up by Berliner Unterwelten in 2006 explains the bunker's layout and fate. It is two minutes from the Holocaust memorial.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.381083,52.512472,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Topography of Terror, Berlin</name><description>Tour day 34

From 1933 the buildings on Prinz-Albrecht-Straße (now Niederkirchnerstraße) housed the Gestapo headquarters, with its notorious house prison at No. 8, and later the Reich Security Main Office (RSHA) formed in September 1939. Here Himmler's SS and police apparatus ran persecution across Germany and occupied Europe, directing the Einsatzgruppen that murdered Jews behind the Eastern Front. The buildings were wrecked by bombing and cleared after the war. The site lay forgotten beside the Berlin Wall until citizens' groups dug out the cellars in the 1980s. The present documentation centre opened in May 2010.

A low grey pavilion holds the main exhibition on the SS and Gestapo. Outside, an open-air trench exhibition runs along excavated cellar walls of the Gestapo buildings, beneath the longest surviving stretch of the outer Berlin Wall on Niederkirchnerstraße.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

