<Placemark><name>German Resistance Memorial (Bendlerblock)</name><description>Tour day 34

The Bendlerblock housed the headquarters of the Replacement Army, where officers planned Operation Valkyrie. On 20 July 1944 Colonel Claus von Stauffenberg planted a bomb at Hitler's headquarters in East Prussia and flew back here to launch the coup. Hitler survived, and by evening loyal troops had regained control. General Friedrich Fromm had the leaders arrested; Stauffenberg, General Friedrich Olbricht, Colonel Albrecht Mertz von Quirnheim and Lieutenant Werner von Haeften were shot in the courtyard that night, and Ludwig Beck was allowed to shoot himself. The memorial centre documents the whole breadth of German resistance.

In the courtyard a bronze figure of a bound young man and a plaque mark the place of the executions. Upstairs, in the rooms used by the plotters, the permanent exhibition covers resistance from churches, workers, students, Jews and the military.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.361389,52.506944,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cecilienhof: the Potsdam Conference</name><description>Tour day 35

From 17 July to 2 August 1945 Truman, Stalin and Churchill (replaced partway through by Clement Attlee after the British general election) met at Cecilienhof, a mock-Tudor country house in Potsdam's New Garden built for the last Crown Prince. It was the third and longest of the wartime summits, organised by the Soviets, who fitted the Great Hall with a round table. The conference settled the occupation of Germany, reparations, the move of Poland's border west to the Oder–Neisse line and the expulsion of Germans from the east, and issued the Potsdam Declaration demanding Japan's surrender.

From outside you can see the half-timbered house in the New Garden and the red star of flowers the Soviets planted in the courtyard. When open, the Great Hall with its round table and the delegations' studies are shown.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.070830,52.419170,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Soviet War Memorial, Treptower Park</name><description>Tour day 35

Opened on 8 May 1949, the fourth anniversary of the German surrender, Treptow is the largest Soviet war memorial outside the former USSR. It commemorates 7,000 of the roughly 80,000 Red Army soldiers killed in the Battle of Berlin; some 5,000 are buried in the central lawns. Architect Yakov Belopolsky laid out the park, and sculptor Yevgeny Vuchetich made the 12-metre bronze of a Soviet soldier holding a German child and a sword, standing on a broken swastika. Sixteen stone sarcophagi, one for each Soviet republic, carry reliefs and quotations from Stalin in Russian and German.

Enter through a granite gateway past the kneeling soldiers, walk down the long axis between the sixteen sarcophagi and the grave lawns, and climb the mound to the soldier statue. Inside its base is a mosaic hall.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.468330,52.487500,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Museum of the Second World War, Gdańsk</name><description>Tour day 37

Opened in March 2017 beside the Motława river, this is one of the largest museums of the war anywhere. The building, by the Gdynia studio Kwadrat, sinks most of its exhibition underground beneath a leaning tower. The permanent exhibition covers the whole war with a strong focus on civilians, occupation and the experience of Poland and Eastern Europe, from the road to war through Westerplatte, occupation terror and the Holocaust to the war's aftermath. It also runs the Westerplatte site. The museum was created by a team led by historian Paweł Machcewicz, and its exhibition became a political battleground after 2016.

Look for the tilted red tower near the Polish Post Office. Inside, the main exhibition is underground and very large: reconstructed streets, vehicles including a Sherman and a T-34, and thousands of objects. Allow at least three hours and pace yourself.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>18.660557,54.356055,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Polish Post Office, Gdańsk</name><description>Tour day 37

The Polish Post Office in the Free City of Danzig was Polish state property and its staff were armed in secret. On 1 September 1939 the 56 people inside, most of them postal workers, held off Danzig police, SS and SA for about 15 hours. The Germans blew a hole in the wall with 600 kg of explosives and then poured petrol into the basement and set it alight. The director, Dr Jan Michoń, was shot as he came out with a white flag, and the rest surrendered. A court-martial condemned the survivors as illegal combatants, and they were shot at Zaspa on 5 October 1939.

The red-brick post office still works as a post office, and the museum branch inside tells the story of the defence with the defenders' personal belongings. Look for bullet and grenade scars on the walls, and the steel monument to the defenders in the square outside.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>18.656852,54.355110,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Stutthof Museum, Sztutowo</name><description>Tour day 38

Stutthof opened on 2 September 1939, the first German concentration camp set up outside Germany's borders in the war. Its first prisoners were Poles from Danzig and Pomerania, especially the educated and community leaders. About 110,000 people from many countries passed through it; around 63,000–65,000 died, including some 28,000 Jews, many of them women brought from the Baltic ghettos and from Auschwitz in 1944. From 1944 the camp also had a gas chamber. The evacuation began on 25 January 1945, and thousands died on the death march. Soviet troops reached the camp only on 9 May 1945. The memorial museum opened in 1962.

Wooden barracks, the commandant's villa, the gas chamber and crematorium, and the 'Death Gate' through which prisoners entered. A large memorial stands by the crematorium. A short documentary film runs hourly in the cinema.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>19.157720,54.329860,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wolf's Lair, Gierłoż near Kętrzyn</name><description>Tour day 38

Organisation Todt built this forest headquarters near Rastenburg in East Prussia, and Hitler moved in on 24 June 1941, two days after the invasion of the Soviet Union began. He spent more than 800 days here, more than anywhere else during the war. On 20 July 1944 Colonel Claus von Stauffenberg left a briefcase bomb at a midday situation conference. Because the meeting was held in a lightly built hut rather than a concrete bunker, the blast was weakened and Hitler survived with minor injuries. He left for the last time on 20 November 1944, and German engineers blew up the bunkers on 24–25 January 1945.

Massive slabs of shattered concrete, some walls more than 2 m thick, lie tilted in the forest. Look for the plaque marking the site of the July 1944 conference hut, Hitler's bunker (No. 13) and Göring's bunker, the best preserved.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>21.498350,54.080470,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Umschlagplatz memorial, Warsaw</name><description>Tour day 40

The Umschlagplatz, a loading yard on Stawki Street beside the railway, was the place from which the Jews of the Warsaw Ghetto were sent to Treblinka. During the Grossaktion Warschau, from 22 July to 21 September 1942, some 254,000–265,000 people were marched here, held in the yard and nearby buildings, and loaded into freight wagons; about 300,000 were deported in all. Among them on 6 August 1942 were Janusz Korczak and the children of his orphanage. The memorial, by Hanna Szmalenberg and Władysław Klamerus, was unveiled on 18 April 1988, on the eve of the uprising's 45th anniversary.

A white marble enclosure about 4 m high, shaped like an open goods wagon. Inside, 400 Polish and Jewish first names are carved, from Aba to Żanna, each standing for a thousand victims. A single tree is visible through the opening.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

