<Placemark><name>State Museum at Majdanek, Lublin</name><description>Tour day 41

Majdanek, on the edge of Lublin, was set up by the SS in autumn 1941 as a prisoner-of-war and then concentration camp, and also served as a killing site for Jews in Operation Reinhard. On 3 November 1943, in Operation Harvest Festival (Aktion Erntefest), SS and police units shot about 18,400 Jews here in a single day, in trenches beside the crematorium, to the sound of loud music. The Red Army reached the camp in late July 1944, and because it was captured nearly intact it was the first major camp seen by Allied forces and reporters. The museum was founded that autumn.

Wooden barracks, watchtowers and fences still stand in open ground within sight of Lublin's housing estates. See the gas chambers and the crematorium. Wiktor Tołkin's 1969 monument stands at the entrance, and the domed mausoleum at the far end holds a mound of victims' ashes.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.606260,51.225320,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sobibór Museum and memorial</name><description>Tour day 41

Sobibór was the second Operation Reinhard death camp, built in forest beside the Chełm–Włodawa railway close to the Bug river. From May 1942 transports brought Jews from the Lublin district and from the Netherlands, Slovakia, Germany and elsewhere; most were gassed within hours. On 14 October 1943 prisoners led by Leon Feldhendler's escape committee and the Soviet-Jewish officer Alexander Pechersky killed several SS men with axes and knives and broke out. About 300 escaped, and roughly 60 survived the war. The Germans closed the camp soon after and planted trees over it.

A modern museum building and memorial landscape in the pine forest. Follow the route of the path the victims were driven along to the gas chambers, whose foundations archaeologists uncovered, and the large area over the mass graves. The old railway station stands nearby.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.593610,51.447220,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bełżec Museum and memorial</name><description>Tour day 42

Bełżec was the first of the three Operation Reinhard death camps, opening on 17 March 1942 beside the Lublin–Lwów railway. Transports brought Jews from the Lublin district, Galicia and Kraków. Within ten months the camp killed some 430,000 to 500,000 people, and the last transport arrived on 11 December 1942. The SS then dug up and burned the bodies and dismantled the camp by mid-1943. Only a handful of prisoners survived; of them only Rudolf Reder, who escaped in late 1942, left a full testimony. The memorial was opened in 2004 as a branch of the Majdanek museum.

The whole camp area is covered with dark industrial slag, marking the mass graves. A path cuts down through it in a narrowing ravine to a wall inscribed with victims' first names. The names of the towns from which transports came line the perimeter. A museum building stands at the entrance.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

