<Placemark><name>Oskar Schindler's Enamel Factory, Kraków</name><description>Tour day 42

In November 1939 the German businessman Oskar Schindler took over the bankrupt Rekord enamelware works at Lipowa 4 in Kraków's Zabłocie district and renamed it Deutsche Emailwarenfabrik (DEF). He first hired Jews from the ghetto because they were cheap, but came to protect them. When the ghetto was destroyed he persuaded Amon Göth, commandant of Płaszów, to let him run a sub-camp at the factory; by 1944 about 1,100 Jews worked here. In autumn 1944 he moved his workers to Brünnlitz in Moravia. About 1,200 people on his lists survived. The factory is now a branch of the Museum of Kraków.

The original gate and administration building, with Schindler's office and a wall of photographs of the people he saved. The main exhibition, 'Kraków under Nazi Occupation 1939–1945', covers the whole city's war: the invasion, the ghetto, Płaszów and resistance, using reconstructed streets and rooms.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

