<Placemark><name>Auschwitz I (main camp), Oświęcim</name><description>Tour day 43

The Germans turned a former Polish army barracks at Oświęcim, renamed Auschwitz, into a concentration camp in 1940. The first mass transport, 728 Polish political prisoners from Tarnów, arrived on 14 June 1940, and Poles were the main prisoners in the early years. In August and September 1941 the SS first tested Zyklon B on Soviet prisoners of war and sick inmates in the cellars of Block 11 and in the old crematorium. Auschwitz I remained the administrative centre of a complex that grew to include Birkenau and Monowitz. The Red Army's 1st Ukrainian Front liberated the camps on 27 January 1945.

Brick blocks behind the 'Arbeit macht frei' gate. The national exhibitions in the blocks hold piles of victims' shoes, suitcases and hair. See Block 11, the 'death block', the execution wall in its yard, and the gas chamber and crematorium beside the SS hospital.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>19.203960,50.026400,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Auschwitz II-Birkenau</name><description>Tour day 43

Construction of Birkenau began in October 1941 at Brzezinka, about 3 km from the main camp. It became the main killing centre of the Holocaust: by June 1943 crematoria II–V were working, with gas chambers in II and III. Most of the roughly 1.1 million people murdered at Auschwitz, about 90 per cent of them Jews, died here. In May 1944 a rail spur and ramp were completed inside the camp for the deportation of Hungary's Jews, who arrived from May to early July. On 7 October 1944 the Sonderkommando rose in revolt and destroyed Crematorium IV. The SS blew up the remaining crematoria before evacuating the camp in January 1945.

Enter through the brick gatehouse, the 'Gate of Death', and see how vast the camp is. Follow the rails to the unloading ramp, then to the ruins of crematoria II and III and the International Monument. Wooden and brick barracks survive; elsewhere only chimneys remain.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>19.180894,50.034386,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Operation Anthropoid Memorial, Prague-Libeň</name><description>Tour day 44

On the morning of 27 May 1942 Reinhard Heydrich was driven in an open Mercedes from his country house towards Prague Castle. At a tight hairpin bend in Libeň, where Kirchmayerova třída (now Zenklova) met V Holešovičkách, Jozef Gabčík stepped out with a Sten gun, which jammed. Jan Kubiš threw a modified anti-tank grenade that burst against the rear wheel, wounding Heydrich; Josef Valčík kept watch further up the road. Both attackers escaped. Heydrich died of his wounds on 4 June. He was the most senior Nazi assassinated during the war, and the reprisals that followed killed thousands of Czechs.

A tall corten-steel column on a traffic island of the modern interchange, with three bronze figures, two soldiers and one civilian, reaching out from its edges, unveiled on 27 May 2009 at 10:35. A bronze slab in front explains the attack. The original bend has been swallowed by the road layout.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>14.465147,50.117856,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Terezín Memorial</name><description>Tour day 45

Terezín, an 18th-century fortress town 60 km north of Prague, served the Nazis in two ways. From June 1940 the Small Fortress across the river was the Prague Gestapo's main prison, holding about 32,000 people. From 24 November 1941 the town itself became the Theresienstadt ghetto, a transit camp for Jews from the Protectorate, Germany, Austria and beyond, including the elderly and prominent. About 33,000 died there of hunger and disease, and about 88,000 were deported on to Auschwitz, Treblinka and other killing sites. In June 1944 the SS staged a 'beautified' ghetto for a Red Cross delegation and later filmed it for propaganda. The Red Army reached Terezín on 8 May 1945.

The Ghetto Museum in the former boys' home, the Magdeburg Barracks with rooms on the ghetto's art, music and theatre, the crematorium and cemetery outside the walls, and across the river the Small Fortress with its cells, courtyards and execution ground. The town is lived in today.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>14.166940,50.513330,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bundeswehr Military History Museum, Dresden</name><description>Tour day 46

Germany's national military museum occupies an arsenal built in 1873–1876 in Dresden's Albertstadt garrison quarter. When it reopened in October 2011, Daniel Libeskind had driven a steel and glass wedge through the old façade. Its tip points towards the former stadium in the Ostragehege, where RAF Pathfinders dropped the target markers for the bombing of 13 February 1945, and the wedge echoes the shape of the bombers' approach over the city. Rather than a parade of weapons, the museum asks about the causes and human cost of war, with strong sections on the Wehrmacht, the Holocaust and the bombing war.

From outside, Libeskind's wedge splitting the neo-classical arsenal. Inside, a chronological route and themed displays in the wedge; climb to its viewing platform 29 metres up and look along the line to the marker point. Special exhibition 'WUNDERWAFFEN' runs to 9 March 2027.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.760633,51.079241,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Colditz Castle</name><description>Tour day 47

Perched above the Mulde, Colditz Castle became Oflag IV-C, a prisoner-of-war camp for Allied officers the Germans classed as escape or security risks. Concentrating persistent escapers in one place produced the opposite of what was intended: tunnels, forged papers, home-made uniforms and one of the best escape records of any camp. In the winter of 1944–45 prisoners built a two-man glider, the Colditz Cock, in a hidden attic workshop, but it never flew. In the last days Himmler ordered prominent prisoners moved away. US troops took the castle on 16 April 1945 after a two-day fight in the town.

Tour the inner courtyard where prisoners exercised, the chapel with the excavated tunnel under its floor, and the museum of escape devices. Guided tours show the routes of famous escapes; a replica of the Colditz Cock has been displayed.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.807500,51.131111,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Torgau: the Elbe Day meeting</name><description>Tour day 47

On 25 April 1945 the US 69th Infantry Division and the Soviet 58th Guards Rifle Division met on the Elbe, cutting Germany in two. The first contact was made that morning near Strehla, some 30 km upstream, by Lieutenant Albert Kotzebue's patrol. At Torgau, Second Lieutenant William Robertson waved a home-made American flag from the tower of Hartenfels Castle; after confusion over signal flares he crossed the wreck of the blown bridge and met Soviet soldiers on it at about 4 pm. The famous handshake photographs were staged on the ruined bridge for the cameras the following day.

On the riverbank stands the Monument to the Meeting, designed by Soviet architect Avraham Miletzki and dedicated in September 1945, beside a viewing platform that is all that remains of the old bridge. Hartenfels Castle, from whose tower Robertson signalled, rises behind.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.009391,51.560048,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nazi Party Rally Grounds &amp; Documentation Centre</name><description>Tour day 49

Six Nazi Party rallies were held at Nuremberg between 1933 and 1938, mass rituals of marching columns, floodlights and speeches designed to show a nation united behind Hitler. Albert Speer designed most of the grounds, including the Zeppelin Field grandstand; the Congress Hall was designed by Ludwig and Franz Ruff. At the 1935 rally the Reichstag, summoned to Nuremberg, passed the Nuremberg Laws that stripped German Jews of citizenship. The Congress Hall was never finished: work stopped in 1943 at about 39 m of a planned 68.5 m, and its interior was never roofed. In April 1945 the US Army blew the giant swastika off the Zeppelin grandstand. The Documentation Centre opened in the north wing of the Congress Hall in 2001.

Inside the Congress Hall the new permanent exhibition explains the rallies, their staging and their victims. Then walk the grounds: the unfinished horseshoe of the Congress Hall, the Great Road, the lake and the crumbling Zeppelin grandstand where Hitler spoke.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.112780,49.433890,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Memorium Nuremberg Trials (Courtroom 600)</name><description>Tour day 49

Nuremberg was chosen for the trial of the major war criminals because its Palace of Justice had survived the bombing largely intact and had a prison attached, and because the city stood for the party rallies. Before the International Military Tribunal of the four Allied powers, 24 leaders were indicted; 22 were tried, Martin Bormann in absentia. The trial opened in Courtroom 600 on 20 November 1945 and the verdicts came on 1 October 1946: 12 death sentences, seven prison terms and three acquittals. Ten men were hanged on 16 October; Göring killed himself the night before. Twelve further US trials of doctors, judges, industrialists and SS officers followed in the same room until 1949.

The courtroom itself, still with its panelling and doors, on the top floor of the Palace of Justice, plus an exhibition on the defendants, the trial and its legacy for international law. A media installation recreates the 1945–46 room layout.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.046500,49.454900,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>NS-Documentation Centre, Munich</name><description>Tour day 50

Munich was where the Nazi Party was founded and where Hitler first made his name; his failed Beer Hall Putsch of 9 November 1923 ended in gunfire at the Feldherrnhalle. After 1933 the regime styled Munich the 'Capital of the Movement'. The party's national headquarters, the Brown House, stood on Brienner Straße beside Königsplatz, flanked by the party's own buildings and 'temples of honour'. The NS-Documentation Centre, opened on 30 April 2015, stands on the site of the Brown House. Its white cube looks out over the surviving party buildings and explains why the movement grew in this city, who supported it, and what it did to its victims.

Four floors of exhibition, read from the top down, with windows framing the surviving Nazi buildings on Königsplatz. Afterwards walk round the square to the Führerbau (now a music school) and the stumps of the Ehrentempel foundations.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>11.567500,48.145280,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Obersalzberg Documentation Centre</name><description>Tour day 51

Hitler rented a chalet on the Obersalzberg above Berchtesgaden in 1928, bought it in 1933 and rebuilt it as the Berghof. Martin Bormann turned the mountainside into a fenced 'Führer zone' with houses for Göring and Bormann, SS barracks and a network of bunkers. It became a second seat of government: Hitler spent much of August 1939 here preparing the attack on Poland, and his last stay ended on 14 July 1944. On 25 April 1945 RAF bombers wrecked most of the buildings, and the SS set fire to the Berghof before US and French troops arrived. Bavaria demolished the ruins in 1952. The Institute of Contemporary History opened the documentation centre in 1999; a new building and exhibition opened in 2023.

The exhibition 'Idyll and Atrocity' links the mountain retreat to the crimes planned from it, then leads down into part of the original bunker system. The Berghof site itself is a short walk away in the woods, with only traces left.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.040280,47.631670,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kehlsteinhaus (Eagle's Nest)</name><description>Tour day 51

Martin Bormann had the Kehlsteinhaus built in 1937–38 just below the Kehlstein summit at about 1,838 m, with a road cut through five tunnels and a 124 m lift shaft inside the mountain. Designed by Roderich Fick, it cost some 30 million Reichsmarks and was presented to Hitler for his 50th birthday in 1939. Hitler, who disliked heights, visited only about 14 times; it was used for receptions and by Eva Braun. The French ambassador André François-Poncet called it the 'eagle's nest' after a visit in October 1938. In early May 1945 French troops of the 2e Division Blindée and US troops reached Berchtesgaden, and the building later served the Allies until 1960.

Ride the bus up the narrow mountain road, walk the tunnel and take the brass-lined lift to the house, now a restaurant with a small history exhibition. On a clear day the views over the Berchtesgaden Alps and towards Salzburg explain the choice of site.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.041670,47.611110,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gusen Memorial</name><description>Tour day 52

Gusen, 5 km west of Mauthausen, opened on 25 May 1940 as a subcamp but at times held more prisoners than the main camp. Polish intellectuals and Spanish Republicans were among the early inmates, worked in granite quarries under murderous conditions. From March 1944 Gusen II housed prisoners who dug the Bergkristall tunnels at St Georgen, where Messerschmitt built Me 262 jet fighter fuselages underground; 987 were completed before May 1945. Official records list 27,842 deaths, but estimates run to 35,000 or more, since many killed on arrival were never registered. US troops of the 11th Armored and 26th Infantry Divisions arrived on 5 May 1945. Most of the camp was later built over by housing.

The memorial built by survivors around the camp's crematorium oven, a small visitor centre with an exhibition, and the surviving gatehouse (the 'Jourhaus', now a private house) among suburban homes. The site is being redesigned as a larger place of remembrance.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>14.464120,48.257030,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Polish War Cemetery, Monte Cassino</name><description>Tour day 53

Polish II Corps under General Władysław Anders attacked the heights north-west of the abbey in May 1944, during Operation Diadem. After a week of fighting on Point 593 and the surrounding ridges, a patrol raised the Polish flag over the abbey ruins on 18 May. The cemetery lies on the slopes of Point 445, the Allied cemetery closest to the abbey. It was designed by Wacław Hryniewicz and Jerzy Skolimowski and consecrated on 1 September 1945. Crosses mark Christian graves and Stars of David mark Jewish ones. Anders, who died in London in 1970, chose to be buried among his men. A Memorial Museum on the 2nd Corps opened nearby in 2014.

Terraced rows of travertine crosses rise to a large stone memorial, with the abbey directly above. Read the Simonides-style inscription ('Passer-by, go tell Poland...') and find General Anders' grave at the front. The small museum shows uniforms, photographs and film.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.805870,41.493840,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cassino War Cemetery and Memorial (CWGC)</name><description>Tour day 53

This Commonwealth cemetery lies on the valley floor below Monte Cassino, among the ground where British, Indian, New Zealand, Canadian and other Commonwealth troops fought between January and May 1944. It holds 4,271 Commonwealth servicemen, 289 of them unidentified. Inside the cemetery stands the Cassino Memorial, which carries the names of more than 4,000 Commonwealth servicemen who died in the Italian campaign and have no known grave. The New Zealand Corps attacks of February and March 1944 and the Eighth Army's crossing of the Rapido (Gari) in May were fought within sight of this spot.

Rows of headstones in a walled garden, with the abbey on its hill above. The Cassino Memorial is a series of green marble panels around a pool. Look up from the cemetery to Monastery Hill to see the slopes the infantry had to climb.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.827100,41.477600,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>German War Cemetery, Cassino (Caira)</name><description>Tour day 53

About 3 km north of Cassino, on the southern foothills of Colle Marino, lies the largest German war cemetery in Italy. In 1959–1960 the Volksbund moved 20,076 German dead here from smaller cemeteries and field graves across the region, including the men who held the Gustav Line around Cassino and those who fought on the Garigliano and in the mountains. Around 3,100 graves have no name. The cemetery opened on 4 May 1965. In 2014 seven plaques were added in the memorial hall for 911 unidentified dead.

Terraces and ellipses of graves climb the hillside, marked by stone crosses carrying several names each. The open-roofed, cube-shaped entrance building lights the sculpture 'Mourning and Consolation' by Suse Müller-Diefenbach. There is a small exhibition and wide views towards Monte Cassino.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>13.822992,41.531300,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Anzio Beachhead Museum (Museo dello Sbarco)</name><description>Tour day 54

On 22 January 1944 US VI Corps under Major General John P. Lucas landed at Anzio and Nettuno in Operation Shingle, to outflank the Gustav Line. US 3rd Infantry Division came ashore at X-Ray beach, British 1st Infantry Division at Peter beach and US Rangers took the port. Lucas consolidated rather than drive inland, and General von Mackensen's Fourteenth Army sealed the beachhead. Major General Lucian Truscott replaced Lucas on 22 February. The breakout, Operation Buffalo, began on 23 May, and Rome fell on 4 June. The museum was founded on 22 January 1994, the 50th anniversary, by a local research association.

A single room in the 17th-century Villa Adele, organised in American, British, German and Italian sections: uniforms, weapons, maps, documents and veterans' photographs. Look for objects raised from the seabed off Anzio, including from HMS Janus, HMS Spartan and the hospital ship St David.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.630587,41.450021,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sicily-Rome American Cemetery, Nettuno</name><description>Tour day 54

On the northern edge of Nettuno, inside the old Anzio beachhead, the American Battle Monuments Commission cemetery holds 7,858 American war dead on 77 acres. Most died in the campaigns from the landing in Sicily on 10 July 1943, through Salerno and the winter fighting, to the Anzio beachhead and the advance on Rome in June 1944. The white marble walls of the chapel carry 3,095 names of the missing. The memorial was designed by Murphy &amp; Locraft and the grounds by Allyn R. Jennings; the cemetery was dedicated in 1956.

Long curving rows of marble crosses and Stars of David on a lawn framed by Roman pines, leading to the chapel and the Tablets of the Missing. The memorial building has large maps of the Sicily and Italian campaigns. Staff in the visitor building can help find a grave.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.658330,41.465280,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fosse Ardeatine Mausoleum, Rome</name><description>Tour day 54

On 23 March 1944 Communist-led GAP partisans bombed a column of the 11th Company, 3rd Battalion, SS Police Regiment 'Bozen' in Via Rasella; 33 policemen eventually died. Hitler authorised a reprisal of ten Italians for each German killed. Herbert Kappler, head of the security police in Rome, drew up the list, using prisoners, Jews and others; Erich Priebke and Karl Hass led the killing squads. On 24 March 335 men and boys, five more than ordered, were shot in disused quarries on the Via Ardeatina, and the tunnels were blown to hide the bodies. The mausoleum, the result of a national competition, was inaugurated on 24 March 1949.

You enter through a gate with bronze sculpture, then walk through the tunnels where the shooting took place. The mausoleum is a vast, low concrete slab over 335 identical tombs, plus one for all Italy's fallen of the liberation war. A small museum explains the events.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.508837,41.858423,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Museum of the Liberation of Rome (Via Tasso)</name><description>Tour day 54

This late-1930s apartment block near St John Lateran was first rented to the German embassy. After the Germans took Rome in September 1943, the SS security police and SD under Herbert Kappler made it their headquarters, with a prison at number 145. More than 2,000 anti-fascists, resisters and Jews passed through its cells; many were tortured, and some were later shot at Forte Bravetta or the Fosse Ardeatine. When the Germans fled in June 1944 they left some prisoners behind. Princess Josepha Ruspoli donated the flats to the state in 1950, and the museum opened on 4 June 1955.

Small former cells on several floors, some kept with prisoners' scratched graffiti on the walls. Displays cover the occupation, the round-up of Rome's Jews, the Resistance and the Ardeatine massacre, with documents, photographs and personal objects.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.506031,41.889565,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>National War Museum, Fort St Elmo, Valletta</name><description>Tour day 55

Fort St Elmo guards the mouth of Grand Harbour at the tip of Valletta. The National War Museum, in the Old Drill Hall of Lower St Elmo, tells the story of the siege of Malta from June 1940 to November 1942. On the first day the island's only fighters were a few obsolete Gloster Sea Gladiators of the Hal Far Fighter Flight, later nicknamed Faith, Hope and Charity. Between 20 March and 28 April 1942 the Luftwaffe flew 11,819 sorties and dropped 6,557 tons of bombs on Malta. On 15 April 1942 King George VI awarded the island the George Cross. The museum reopened in 2015 after the fort's restoration.

The fuselage of Sea Gladiator N5520 'Faith', the George Cross itself, and the Willys jeep 'Husky' used by Eisenhower and by Roosevelt on his visit to Malta. The wider museum covers Malta's military history; the star-shaped fort walls overlook Grand Harbour.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>14.518901,35.901159,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Malta at War Museum, Birgu</name><description>Tour day 55

Birgu faces Valletta across Grand Harbour, beside the dockyard that was a prime target throughout the siege. The museum occupies the Couvre Porte, a 17th-century counterguard whose 18th-century casemates became barracks. During the war they served as a police headquarters and then a civil defence centre. In 1940 the police began digging an air-raid shelter into the rock below, which grew into a small underground village where the town's people slept, gave birth and sheltered from raids. The Luftwaffe's arrival in Sicily in January 1941, when Ju 87s bombed HMS Illustrious in the harbour, brought the war to Birgu's doorstep. The museum is run by Fondazzjoni Wirt Artna.

Above ground, nine galleries of wartime memorabilia and the wartime documentary 'Malta G.C.'. Below, a rock-cut shelter with dormitories, a surgery, a birth room, family cubicles and long tunnels. Allow time for the hourly English guided tour.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>14.522667,35.885417,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Galatas, Crete: the battle for the village</name><description>Tour day 56

Galatas, a hill village south-west of Chania, anchored the line held by Colonel Howard Kippenberger's 10th Brigade and later by 4th New Zealand Brigade. On 25 May 1941 German mountain and parachute troops attacked after heavy bombing. A covering force on Pink Hill held out until overrun, and Brigadier Inglis threw in 23rd Battalion and improvised troops, including bandsmen and the Kiwi Concert Party. The Germans entered Galatas. That evening Kippenberger sent two companies of 23rd Battalion and the remnants of 18th Battalion in behind two light tanks of 3rd Hussars under Lieutenant Roy Farran. They charged into the square and drove the Germans out, but were too weak to stay and withdrew that night.

The village square where the bayonet charge ended, the Allied memorial on the edge of the village and views to Pink Hill and the former 'Prison Valley' to the south. A small local museum covers the battle. Annual commemorations are held here each May.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.966326,35.491923,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Souda Bay War Cemetery, Crete</name><description>Tour day 56

In May 1941 Commonwealth forces on Crete held five defence areas along the north coast. After the German airborne landings began on 20 May, Maleme fell and the line west of Chania broke on 26 May. Souda Bay became indefensible, and the troops retreated over the mountains to Sfakia. Between 28 and 31 May the Royal Navy evacuated about 18,000 men from Crete, most of them from Sfakia. Of 32,000 Commonwealth soldiers on the island, about 12,000 were captured and 2,000 killed. After the war, Australian War Graves Units moved the dead here from German-made burial grounds at Chania, Heraklion, Rethymno and Galatas and from isolated graves. About 1,500 Second World War servicemen lie here, 776 unidentified.

A quiet, walled cemetery in an olive grove on the shore of Souda Bay, designed by Louis de Soissons. Rows of headstones run down towards the water. Look for the grave of the archaeologist and SOE officer John Pendlebury and for the many headstones marked 'Known unto God'.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>24.060457,35.500162,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Norway's Resistance Museum, Akershus Fortress</name><description>Tour day 57

Norway's Resistance Museum (Norges Hjemmefrontmuseum) stands inside Akershus Fortress in Oslo, which the Germans used during the occupation. It tells the story from the invasion of 9 April 1940 to the liberation in May 1945: the fighting of 1940, the government in exile in London, Quisling's collaborationist regime, civil resistance by teachers, clergy and sportsmen, the underground press, the Milorg military resistance and sabotage operations such as Vemork. It also covers the deportation of Norway's Jews in 1942. Resistance members were executed by the Germans at the fortress, and a memorial stands near the museum.

A chronological walk through the occupation using dioramas, documents, weapons, radios and underground newspapers. Outside, the fortress walls and the memorial to executed Norwegian patriots. The fortress grounds give views over Oslo harbour.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>10.735800,59.907900,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Narvik War Museum (Narviksenteret)</name><description>Tour day 58

Narvik mattered for its ice-free port, which shipped Swedish iron ore by rail. On 9 April 1940 German destroyers landed General Eduard Dietl's mountain troops; the Norwegian coastal defence ships Eidsvold and Norge were sunk and the town surrendered. On 10 April Commodore Bernard Warburton-Lee's destroyers attacked the harbour, sinking two German destroyers; he was killed and awarded a posthumous VC. On 13 April a force led by HMS Warspite destroyed the remaining German destroyers. After weeks of mountain fighting, Norwegian, French and Polish troops retook Narvik on 28 May, but the Allies withdrew in early June and Dietl reoccupied the town on 8 June.

A modern museum in the centre of Narvik, sharing a building with the public library since 2016. Exhibitions cover the 1940 campaign and the occupation years to 1945. Look for Warburton-Lee's Victoria Cross and a German Enigma machine.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>17.425375,68.437776,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Brest Fortress, Belarus (June 1941)</name><description>Tour day 59

At 04:15 Moscow time on 22 June 1941 German artillery, including 60 cm mortars, opened Operation Barbarossa against this 19th-century fortress on the Bug. The attackers were the Austrian 45th Infantry Division under Fritz Schlieper, about 17,000 men. Inside were around 9,000 Soviet troops from the 6th and 42nd Rifle Divisions and NKVD border guards, plus families. The Germans expected one day; it took about a week. Captain Ivan Zubachyov and Commissar Yefim Fomin led the citadel's resistance; Fomin was shot after capture. The East Fort surrendered on 29 June after Luftwaffe bombing. Major Pyotr Gavrilov, wounded, was taken on 23 July. About 6,800 Soviet soldiers were captured.

Enter through the star-shaped main gate cut into the earth rampart, then walk to the huge stone head of the 'Courage' monument and the 100 m bayonet obelisk. Look for the battered Kholm and Terespol gates, the ruined barracks, the 'Thirst' sculpture and the defence museum in the old barracks ring.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.656852,52.082411,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Khatyn Memorial, Belarus</name><description>Tour day 59

On 22 March 1943, after a partisan ambush nearby, Schutzmannschaft Battalion 118, made up largely of Ukrainian and other Soviet collaborators, and the SS Dirlewanger unit surrounded the village of Khatyn, about 50 km north of Minsk. They herded the villagers into a barn, set it alight and shot those who broke out, then burned the village. About 149 people were killed, around 75 of them children. The blacksmith Yuzif Kaminsky was the only adult survivor. In 1969 the site became the Byelorussian SSR's national war memorial, designed by Yuri Gradov, Valentin Zankovich and Leonid Levin, and it stands for the hundreds of Belarusian villages destroyed in anti-partisan operations.

Concrete outlines of the 26 houses, each with a chimney-shaped bell tower ringing in turn, the bronze 'Unbowed Man' of Kaminsky carrying his dead son, the stylised barn roof over the mass grave, the 'Cemetery of Villages' with 185 symbolic graves, and the wall naming camps and extermination sites in Belarus.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.943611,54.334444,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Piskaryovskoye Cemetery, St Petersburg</name><description>Tour day 61

During the siege of Leningrad, from September 1941 to 27 January 1944, the city's dead were brought here by lorry and sledge and buried in trenches. The cemetery holds about 420,000 civilians, most of whom died of starvation and cold, and about 50,000 soldiers of the Leningrad Front, in 186 mass graves. Each grave is marked only with the year of burial. The memorial, designed by Alexander Vasilyev and Yevgeny Levinson, opened on 9 May 1960. Its centrepiece is the bronze figure of the Motherland by Vera Isayeva and Robert Taurit. On the granite wall behind her is a poem by Olga Berggolts, the poet whose radio broadcasts kept the besieged city company.

Begin at the eternal flame between two small pavilions with the exhibition, then walk down the 480 m central avenue between the low grassy mounds of the mass graves, each with a granite slab giving only a year and a hammer and sickle or a star. The Motherland statue and Berggolts's wall close the view.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>30.422602,59.996534,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Museum of the Defence and Siege of Leningrad</name><description>Tour day 61

This museum began while the siege was still on: an exhibition, 'Heroic Defence of Leningrad', opened in the Solyanoy Gorodok halls on 30 April 1944, and it became the Leningrad Defence Museum on 27 January 1946, the second anniversary of the siege being lifted. In 1949 it was closed during the 'Leningrad Affair', Stalin's purge of the city's wartime leadership; its managers were persecuted, it was liquidated in 1952, and weapons were melted down. It reopened in the same building on 8 September 1989. It covers the 872 days of encirclement, the Road of Life across Lake Ladoga and daily life on bread rations that fell to 125 g for dependants.

The main exhibition is in one large hall on the upper floor: weapons, uniforms, posters and models, a big map of the Road of Life, a recreated siege-time room with a small iron stove, and a display of the 125 g bread ration. Labels are mostly in Russian; an audio guide is offered.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>30.340900,59.944500,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mamayev Kurgan and 'The Motherland Calls'</name><description>Tour day 62

Marked on military maps as Height 102.0, this hill overlooks central Stalingrad and the Volga crossings, and whoever held it could see and shell the river. From mid-September 1942 it changed hands repeatedly as the German 6th Army tried to reach the Volga and Chuikov's 62nd Army clung on. Fighting on the slopes went on until 26 January 1943, when Soviet forces advancing from the west broke through to the defenders. The memorial ensemble was built from 1959 and 'The Motherland Calls', 85 m to the tip of her sword, was unveiled on 15 October 1967. Yevgeny Vuchetich sculpted it; Nikolai Nikitin engineered it. About 34,505 defenders are buried on the hill, and Marshal Chuikov chose to be buried here.

From the avenue at the foot, climb past the Square of Those Who Fought to the Death and the ruined-wall reliefs, to the circular Hall of Military Glory with its eternal flame and changing guard. Above, the vast sword-raising Motherland stands among the mass graves; Chuikov's grave is near her base.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.536944,48.742500,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Panorama Museum &amp; Gerhardt's Mill, Volgograd</name><description>Tour day 62

The Panorama Museum on the Volga embankment is the main museum of the Battle of Stalingrad, with eight halls, four dioramas, a circular panorama painting and about 4,000 exhibits, among them the rifle of the sniper Vasily Zaitsev. Beside it stands the shell of Gerhardt's (or Grudinin's) mill. Bombed and set on fire on 14 September 1942, it was taken that night by men of General Rodimtsev's 13th Guards Rifle Division, newly ferried across the Volga. By 20 September the 3rd Battalion, 42nd Guards Rifle Regiment, held it as a strongpoint, and it stayed semi-encircled for 58 days. It was deliberately left unrestored after the war.

Start with the scarred, roofless mill, its brick walls pitted by bullets and shells, then go into the museum for the dioramas, Zaitsev's rifle and the panorama on the top floor. Pavlov's House is across the square, which shows how close the lines were.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.532731,48.715036,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pavlov's House, Volgograd</name><description>Tour day 62

This four-storey 1930s block on what was 9 January Square was seized in late September 1942 by a group under Sergeant Yakov Pavlov of the 42nd Guards Rifle Regiment, 13th Guards Rifle Division. Reinforced to about 31 men under Lieutenant Ivan Afanasyev, they mined the approaches, set up machine guns and fired an anti-tank rifle from the roof, holding the house until the Soviet counter-offensive on 25 November. Three of the garrison were killed. The house became the best-known symbol of Stalingrad's street fighting, and General Chuikov later joked that the Germans lost more men attacking it than they did taking Paris.

The rebuilt house is still lived in. On the end wall facing Sovetskaya Street is the memorial wall of 1985, built of bricks gathered after the battle, with a soldier relief and the garrison's names. Gerhardt's Mill and the Panorama Museum stand across the square.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Private land — view from the road.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.531654,48.715969,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rossoshka war cemeteries</name><description>Tour day 62

Rossoshka lies in the steppe about 37 km north-west of central Volgograd, in the middle of the pocket where the German 6th Army was encircled from November 1942 until its surrender in early February 1943. The villages of Bolshaya and Malaya Rossoshka were destroyed in the fighting. Two cemeteries face each other across the road. The Soviet cemetery opened on 23 August 1997 and holds about 18,520 Red Army dead, mostly unknown. The German cemetery, built by the Volksbund and dedicated in 1999, holds over 61,000 German soldiers, and granite cubes dedicated in 2006 carry the names of nearly 120,000 missing. A Chapel of Peace opened in 2016.

On the German side, a circular enclosure about 150 m across with a central cross, and beyond it rows of tall granite cubes covered in names. Across the road, the Soviet cemetery with Vysotsky's lines on the entrance walls and the 'Mother in Sorrow' holding a bell without a clapper.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.165319,48.825447,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Prokhorovka battlefield, Belgorod region</name><description>Tour day 63

On 12 July 1943, during the German Kursk offensive (Operation Citadel), Pavel Rotmistrov's 5th Guards Tank Army counter-attacked Paul Hausser's II SS Panzer Corps on the fields south-west of Prokhorovka, about 87 km south-east of Kursk. Some 500 Soviet tanks attacked in two waves against 294 German tanks and assault guns. The Germans stopped the attack, but the Red Army stopped the German advance; Hitler ended Citadel on 13 July, partly because the Allies had landed in Sicily. Soviet losses were heavy: Valery Zamulin counts 340 tanks and self-propelled guns, against about 60–80 German armoured vehicles damaged or destroyed. A memorial bell tower was built in 1995 and the 'Third Military Field of Russia' museum in 2010.

The 52 m white bell tower stands on the open field with its bells ringing every 20 minutes. Look for the 'Tank Battle' sculpture of colliding tanks in front of the museum, the Church of Sts Peter and Paul, a tank museum, preserved Soviet armour, and the long rolling fields where the armour met.

Accuracy: AREA — centre of a wider area.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.672313,51.017354,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kursk Arc memorial complex, Kursk</name><description>Tour day 63

The Battle of Kursk, from 5 July to 23 August 1943, began as Germany's last great offensive in the east, Operation Citadel, aimed at cutting off the Soviet salient around Kursk from north and south. Deep Soviet defences halted it within a week, and Soviet counter-offensives went on to take Orel and Kharkov. This memorial complex on Prospekt Pobedy opened in 1998 for the battle's 55th anniversary. It includes a mass grave with an eternal flame, a bronze statue of Marshal Georgy Zhukov unveiled on 5 July 1998, the Church of St George, a 24 m triumphal arch of 2000 crowned by St George slaying the dragon, and an alley of wartime weapons.

A 600 m avenue lined by the triumphal arch with its soldier statues, the gilded-domed church of St George, the Zhukov statue, the mass grave and eternal flame, and an open-air line of equipment including a T-34, an ISU-152 and a Katyusha rocket launcher.

Accuracy: Documented site.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/footsteps-of-wwii/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</description><styleUrl>#s1</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.170674,51.787353,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
